    "title": "How to watch Spurs playoff Game 1 vs Trail Blazers tonight",
    "text": "The Spurs host the Trail Blazers in Game 1 at Frost Bank Center.Billy CaddenFor the first time since the 2019 postseason, the San Antonio Spurs are officially back in the playoffs, and they’re set to begin their postseason run Sunday at Frost Bank Center against the Portland Trail Blazers. After finishing the regular season with an impressive 62-20 record, San Antonio secured the No. 2 seed in the Western Conference and now gets home-court advantage to start the playoffs. Portland earned its spot the hard way, taking down the Suns in the 7/8 Play-In game to lock in the No. 7 seed and bring a 42-40 record into this matchup. On paper, the Spurs are heavy favorites, and that’s reflected in the 10.5-point spread heading into Game 1.All eyes will be on Victor Wembanyama, who has played at an MVP level all season and established himself as easily the most dominant defensive presence in the league. But as talented as this Spurs team is, this will be the first playoff experience for both Wembanyama and rising star Stephon Castle, adding a tinge of nerves and uncertainty to Sunday’s opener. Veteran playoff performer De’Aaron Fox will be key in keeping the offense steady and composed as the young core adjusts to the postseason stage. With expectations sky high in San Antonio, Game 1 is the first step in what the team hopes will be a deep playoff run.The Spurs vs Trail Blazers game tips off Sunday night at 8:00 p.m. CST, and fans here in San Antonio can catch all the action with Peacock.When is Spurs vs Trail Blazers Game 1? What: San Antonio Spurs vs Portland Trail Blazers — First Round Game 1 When: Sunday, April 19, 2026, at 8:00 p.m. CST Where: Frost Bank Center, San Antonio, TX TV: NBC, Peacock How to stream the Spurs vs Blazers Game 1 without cableIf you’ve looked at the playoff broadcasting schedule for any team or series, you’ve seen just how much it jumps all over the place between networks and streaming services. And that’s no different for the Spurs, with Sunday’s game airing on NBC and Peacock before the remaining first-round games are broadcast elsewhere. For Sunday’s game specifically, the easiest way for Spurs fans to catch the action is to just sign up for Peacock.The good thing about Peacock is that quite a few playoff games this postseason will air on NBC and Peacock, so it’s worth signing up for no matter what. That’s especially true here in San Antonio because our top streaming service, Sling TV, does not carry NBC in the local area. So while we always suggest using Sling when you can, Spurs fans might be better off signing up for any of the below streaming services this postseason: fuboTV ($48.99 for the first month, then $73.99/month with a 7-day free trial) DIRECTV STREAM ($89.99/month; no free trials available at this time) Hulu + Live TV ($89.99/month with a 3-day free trial; also includes Disney+ and ESPN+) YouTube TV ($67.99/month for the first three months, then $82.99/month with a 5-day free trial) That said, there are a lot of Spurs fans outside the San Antonio metro, and Sling TV is still our top choice for watching the playoffs, so be sure to check your zip code to see what’s offered. To catch as many Spurs playoff games as possible, you’ll need access to ABC, NBC, and ESPN, all of which are included in the Sling Orange & Blue plan in most areas, and it starts at just $60.99/month, depending on your location. Here are the best ways for fans outside of the local area to catch Sunday’s game and most of the Spurs playoff games: Sling TV 3-Month Prepay ($49.99 - $159.99/month then monthly recurring payments) Sling TV Select ($19.99/month) Sling TV Blue ($45.99/month) Sling TV Orange & Blue ($60.99/month) Keep in mind that Amazon Prime Video also has exclusive streaming rights to about one-third of the first and second round games this season, including Game 3 of this series. So, in addition to one of the streaming platforms above, you’ll also need to sign up for Prime Video to keep up with the Spurs this postseason.April 19, 2026Andrew Koopman is a freelance writer who's been producing content since 2020. If he's not writing, Andrew is almost certainly watching sports (go Warriors!) or planning his next adventure as he attempts to visit all 50 states in the next few years.",
